Hand in hand, we gaze through tear-blurred eyes;  
speech fails, and we choke on silence.  
I think of the road stretching on and on—  
a thousand miles of misty waters;  
heavy dusk spreads beneath the vast Chu sky.

Rain slants, wild winds rage at March’s end;
At dusk I close the door—no way to keep spring here.
Through tearful eyes I ask the flowers;
They answer not. Fallen blossoms whirl past the swing.

White dew forms on the jade steps;
late in the night it dampens silk stockings.
The crystal curtain is lowered;
through its clear shimmer, the autumn moon is gazed upon.

I cherish the quiet grass that grows beside the stream;
Above, orioles sing deep among the trees.
At dusk, the spring tide rushes in with rain;
At the deserted ferry, a boat lies crosswise on its own.

I cherish most the secluded grass beside the stream;
Above, orioles sing deep among the trees.
The spring tide rushes in with rain at dusk;
At the deserted ferry, a boat lies crosswise, alone.

I love the quiet grass beside the stream;  
above it, an oriole sings in the deep woods.  
At dusk, the spring tide, swollen with rain, runs swift;  
at the deserted ferry,  
a boat lies crosswise on its own.

I love the quiet grasses growing by the stream;
Above, an oriole sings deep in the trees.
Spring tides rise with the rain, swift in the evening;
At the deserted ferry, a boat drifts crosswise alone.

I cherish the quiet grasses growing beside the ravine;
Above them, orioles sing from the deep woods.
At dusk, spring tides surge swiftly with the rain;
At the deserted ferry, a boat lies crosswise on its own.

I cherish most the hidden grass beside the stream;
Above, an oriole sings deep among the trees.
Spring tide and rain come rushing in at dusk;
At the empty country ferry, a boat lies crosswise, alone.

I cherish the quiet grasses by the stream;
Above, orioles sing in the deep woods.
The spring tide, rain-laden, surges at dusk;
At the deserted ferry, the boat drifts crosswise alone.

I cherish the quiet grasses growing by the stream;
Above, an oriole calls from deep within the trees.
The spring tide, swollen by rain, rushes in at dusk;
At the wild ferry, no one is there—the boat lies crosswise on its own.

I cherish most the quiet grass beside the stream;
Above, an oriole sings deep among the trees.
At dusk, the spring tide rushes in with rain;
At the deserted country ferry, a boat lies crosswise on the water.

I cherish the quiet grass beside the stream;
above, in deep woods, orioles sing.
The spring tide, rain-swollen, surges at dusk;
at the wild ferry, no one—one boat drifts crosswise.

I cherish the hidden grasses by the stream;
Above, an oriole sings deep in the trees.
At dusk, the spring tide rushes with the rain;
No one at the lonely crossing—a boat lies crosswise.

I love the secluded grasses growing beside the stream;
Above them, orioles call from the depths of the trees.
At dusk, spring’s rising waters, swollen with rain, rush on;
At the deserted ferry, an empty boat lies crosswise.

I love the quiet grasses by the stream;
Above, an oriole calls from the deep woods.
Spring tides, swollen with rain, rush in at dusk;
At the empty ferry, a boat drifts crosswise.

I cherish only the quiet grass beside the stream;
Above, an oriole sings deep among the trees.
At dusk, the spring tide, borne with rain, runs swift;
At the deserted ferry, a boat lies crosswise on its own.

I alone cherish the quiet grass growing beside the ravine;
above, an oriole calls from deep within the trees.
The spring tide, swollen by rain, rushes in at dusk;
at the deserted ferry, a boat drifts crosswise on its own.

At dawn, the journey bells stir;
A traveler grieves for his native land.
Cockcrow, the thatched inn beneath the moon;
Footprints mark the frosted plank bridge.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Poncirus flowers brighten the posthouse wall.
Then I dream of Duling once more—
Ducks and geese fill the returning pond.

At dawn, the travel bells stir;  
A traveler, sorrowing for his home.  
Cockcrow, a thatched inn, and moonlight;  
Footprints on the plank bridge, frost-white.  
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;  
Trifoliate orange blossoms brighten the post-station wall.  
Thinking of dreams of Duling,  
Wild ducks and geese fill the returning pond.

At dawn the travel bells stir;  
on the road, I grieve for home.  
Cockcrow, a thatched inn beneath the moon;  
footprints on the bridge, frost on its planks.  
Oak leaves fall along the mountain path;  
zithorn blossoms brighten the post-station wall.  
And so I dream of Duling:  
wild ducks and geese fill the winding pond.

At dawn the travel bells stir;
A wanderer grieves for home.
Cockcrow, an inn beneath the moon;
Footprints on the frosted plank bridge.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Blossoms of trifoliate orange brighten the post-station wall.
And so I think of my dream of Duling: ducks and geese
Filling the pond by the winding bank.

Rising at dawn, I hear the journey’s bells;
Far from home, I travel on in sorrow.
Cockcrow, a thatched inn, the moon;
Footprints, a plank bridge, the frost.
Oak leaves lie fallen on the mountain road;
Bitter-orange blossoms brighten the post-house wall.
I think of my dream of Duling—
Wild ducks and geese filling the winding pond.

Rising at dawn, I set the journey bells ringing;
Far from home, I grieve for my native place.
Cockcrow, a thatched inn, the moon;
Footprints, a plank bridge, the frost.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
White orange blossoms brighten the post-house wall.
Then I recall my dream of Duling:
Wild ducks and geese fill the curving pond.

At dawn, travel bells stir me awake;
A wayfarer, I grieve for home.
Cockcrow, the thatched inn beneath the moon;
Footprints, frost on the plank bridge.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Pummelo blossoms brighten the post-station wall.
Thinking of Duling, I dream—
Ducks and geese fill the winding pond.

At dawn, the travel bells stir;
A homesick traveler sets forth.
Cockcrow, the thatched inn under moonlight;
Footprints on the plank bridge, bright with frost.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Poncirus blossoms brighten the courier wall.
And thinking of a dream of Duling—
Wild ducks and geese fill the returning pond.

At dawn, the travel bells stir;
As I journey, I grieve for my old home.
Cockcrow, a thatched inn, the moon;
Footprints on the plank bridge, frost.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Poncirus blossoms brighten the courier station wall.
Thinking of my dream of Duling,
I see wild ducks and geese filling the winding pond.

Rising at dawn, I set the journey bells ringing;
Far from home, I grieve for home.
Cockcrow, moonlight on the thatched inn;
Footprints in frost on the plank bridge.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Bitter-orange blossoms brighten the post-house wall.
Then I recall my dream of Duling:
Wild ducks and geese crowd a curving pond.

At dawn, the journey bells begin to stir;
A traveler grieves for his old home.
Cockcrow, a thatched inn, the moon;
Frost on the plank bridge, footprints passing.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
Trifoliate-orange flowers brighten the courier wall.
Then I recall my dream of Duling—
Wild ducks and geese fill the returning pond.
